### What is strong consistency?

Strong consistency means that once a write commits, any subsequent read anywhere returns that value or newer. Achieving it requires coordination such as a single leader, quorum reads and writes, or synchronous replication, all of which add latency.

```java
package com.bank.consistency;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class StrongConsistencyRead {
    public BigDecimal balanceStrong(String accountId) {
        return leader.read(accountId);  // always the latest committed value
    }
}
```

### What is eventual consistency?

Eventual consistency relaxes the guarantee. A write is propagated asynchronously to replicas, so for a short window different replicas may hold different values, but they all converge. This allows low-latency, highly available reads from any replica.

```java
package com.bank.consistency;

public class EventualConsistencyRead {
    public long likeCountEventual(String postId) {
        return nearestReplica.read(postId);  // may lag; converges shortly
    }
}
```

### What is read-your-writes consistency?

Read-your-writes guarantees a user sees their own writes immediately even if others see them slightly later. I implement this by routing a user's own reads to the leader right after they write.

```java
package com.bank.consistency;

public class ReadYourWrites {
    public String read(String query, boolean justWrote) {
        return justWrote ? leader.query(query) : anyReplica.query(query);
    }
}
```

### What is tunable consistency?

Tunable consistency, offered by databases like Cassandra, lets you choose per operation how many replicas must acknowledge. Write to a quorum and read from a quorum, and you get strong consistency. Write to one and read from one, and you get eventual consistency with lower latency.

```java
package com.bank.consistency;

public class TunableConsistency {
    // Quorum: W + R > N guarantees strong consistency
    // N=3, W=2, R=2 -> strong (2+2 > 3)
    // N=3, W=1, R=1 -> eventual (1+1 < 3)
    public String consistencyLevel(String dataType) {
        return switch (dataType) {
            case "balance" -> "QUORUM (strong)";
            case "like_count" -> "ONE (eventual, fast)";
            default -> "QUORUM (safe default)";
        };
    }
}
```

### How does the domain decide the choice?

| Data type | Consistency | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Account balance | Strong | Wrong value = double-spend |
| Transaction ledger | Strong | Append-only, audit-critical |
| Social feed likes | Eventual | Brief staleness harmless |
| Analytics counters | Eventual | Approximate is fine |
| Dashboard metrics | Eventual | Seconds of lag acceptable |

### What is the cost of strong consistency?

Strong consistency requires coordination, which adds latency and can reduce availability during partitions (CAP theorem). I apply it only where a wrong or stale value causes real harm.

### What are the middle grounds?

Between strong and eventual are read-your-writes (user sees own changes), causal consistency (preserves cause-effect), and session consistency (monotonic reads within a session).

### What should a senior engineer know?

Consistency is a per-data-type decision, not a single global setting. The same bank uses strong consistency for the ledger and eventual for the community feed. Deliberately spending strong consistency only where needed and relaxing everywhere else gains speed, scale, and availability.
